A customer-managed KMS key means a KMS key that YOU create and control, instead of using the AWS-managed key that AWS provides automatically.

Why do we need it?

The default AWS-managed key is convenient, but you have less control.

With a customer-managed key, you can control:

Who can use the key for encryption/decryption
Who can manage the key
Key policy
Key rotation
Disable/enable the key
Delete the key
Audit key usage
Example

Suppose your company has two applications:

Application A
   ↓
Secret A
   ↓
KMS Key A 🔑

Application B
   ↓
Secret B
   ↓
KMS Key B 🔑

You can make sure the role for Application A can use Key A, but cannot use Key B.

App A → Key A → ✅
App A → Key B → ❌

That's the main reason for using customer-managed keys: more control over the encryption key and access to it.

Default vs Customer-managed
AWS-managed KMS key	Customer-managed KMS key
AWS creates/manages it	You create/manage it
Easy to use	More control
Less configuration	More configuration
Good for normal use	Useful for strict security/compliance requirements
Interview answer

A customer-managed KMS key is a KMS key created and managed by the customer. We use it when we need greater control over key policies, permissions, rotation, disabling, deletion, and auditing.



